upstream_title: Goldbach30 / Goldbach29 FRACTRAN reduction pair and halting-semantics provenance

attribution_boundary:
  Mike Dupont / meta-introspector owns the upstream Goldbach30 and Goldbach29
  constructions, their 30-to-29 fraction reduction, abstract machines,
  FRACTRAN programs, and Lean proofs fstep_enc. The later Goldbach29 source
  visibly contains theorem text goldbachProg_halts_iff, but the pinned semantic
  tree does not earn build-verified theorem authority because Main.lean and
  Loops.lean are the same self-importing blob. The Goldbach30 commit narrative
  claims a matching theorem, but DASHI does not promote that narrative to formal
  theorem authority because the claimed Main.lean/Loops.lean files are absent.
  DASHI owns the provenance/audit receipts, independent observer/fibre
  translation, source-faithful arithmetic reconstruction, local tail quotient,
  checkpoint theorem, and source-driven replay. Vendored upstream files retain
  Mike/meta-introspector attribution and AGPL-3.0 licensing.

literal_rule_delta_at_construction_commit:
  - rules 1 through 25 are identical annotated rules in both Program.lean blobs
  - Goldbach30 rule 26 is NT.B[f] -> SA
  - Goldbach30 rule 27 is NT.B[b,c] -> RA.A[a,d^2,f]
  - Goldbach30 rules 28..30 are SA[a] -> SB[b,c], SB -> SA, SA[c] -> RA.A[a^2,d^2,f]
  - Goldbach29 rule 26 is NT.B[f,a] -> SB[b,c]
  - Goldbach29 rule 27 is NT.B[f] -> NT2.B[a,b]
  - Goldbach29 rule 28 is the same failure rule NT.B[b,c] -> RA.A[a,d^2,f]
  - Goldbach29 rule 29 is SB -> NT.B[f]
  - therefore all post-prefix differences are confined to the success routing;
    the failure transition is retained literally with only its list position shifted

source_reachable_success_guard:
  - NT.B success is reached only after earlier s/g restoration branches are exhausted
  - exact DASHI macro contract therefore fixes s=0, g=0 and verdict f=1
  - tested a is structurally positive
  - c may be zero on entry; canonical first success has a=2,b=2,c=0,f=1
  - arbitrary f>1 is intentionally excluded because Goldbach29 would re-enter
    the success branch after staging rather than satisfy the one-pass quotient

external_facts_used:
  - Goldbach30 has 30 fractions and 11 control states
  - Goldbach29 has 29 fractions and 10 control states
  - the first 25 annotated rules are shared before the success/failure tail
  - the SA control state is removed in Goldbach29
  - Goldbach30 success uses NT.B -> SA, the SA/SB transfer loop, then SA -> RA.A
  - Goldbach29 success reroutes the transfer through NT.B/SB, stages through
    NT2.B, and reuses the existing NT.B b c -> RA.A transition
  - each committed Program.lean proves one encoded FRACTRAN step matches one abstract astep step (fstep_enc)
  - Goldbach29 semantic source text contains goldbachProg_halts_iff but its duplicate/self-import tree prevents promotion to build-verified authority at the pinned revision
  - Goldbach30 commit 7added5000a75226a834ba7aab08a7380f0c70b3 narratively claims the corresponding Goldbach30 theorem and no-sorry build
  - recursive tree audit and GitHub path-history queries recover no Goldbach30/Main.lean or Goldbach30/Loops.lean

path_provenance_note:
  The construction commit itself records Goldbach29 under the d3e01240 UUID
  directory. A later/user-supplied link used another UUID path; DASHI does not
  rewrite commit-native provenance to that later path.

non_claims:
  - vendoring does not transfer authorship to DASHI
  - byte-identical Program source is not cross-prover proof transport
  - source-visible theorem text is not a clean kernel-build receipt
  - a commit-message theorem claim without its source artifact is not formal theorem authority
  - the DASHI checkpoint theorem is not yet a formal same-object proof about the pinned Lean/FRACTRAN carriers
  - goldbachProg_halts_iff text is an equivalence to a counterexample; it is not a proof that no counterexample exists and therefore does not prove Goldbach
  - whole-machine weak/stuttering bisimulation remains unpaid and optional for richer fine-state consumers
